  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nConvection from this morning and early afternoon will complicate\nadditional severe chances into later this afternoon and this\nevening. From a mesoscale perspective, we would expect\nsubsidence on the backside of the passing MCV, and plentiful\ncloud cover may stable cold pools around for the afternoon\nacross much of the area. Realistically, the only able that may\nbe able to clear and destabilize again would most likely be\ninto Ohio, where models like the HRRR initiate and back-build \non the western periphery for this morning cold pool. The severe\nthreat with this would remain relatively low, but the flood \nthreat could be moderate should thia actually realize. In the \nevent that this does not, hazards are likely over through this \nevening.\n\nOvernight tonight, CAMS are again n bi-modal on any convective\ndevelopment. In ones that do convect, it seems like eastern Ohio\ninto northern West Virginia is the favored development zone.\nWith the potential for training storms and heavy rainfall \nrates, flash flooding is the primary risk. Severe chances will \nremain relatively lower, with most HREF sounding showing the \ndevelopment of a near-surface stable layer with nocturnal \ncooling. All this to say, large hail or winds couldn't be rules\nout should cells grow particularly strong, though this is not \nanticipated at this time.\n\nDestabilization is again expected into Tuesday with more storm\nchances. Apart from run-of-the mill downburst and flood threats\nwith PWATS of ~1.6 inches and DCAPE near 800, we will have to\nmonitor the potential for MCS passages into Tuesday evening.\nCAMS hint on development in Ohio Tuesday afternoon/evening, with\nlow confidence in exact location. Should is run over eastern \nOhio, wind would be the primary threat, and tornadoes / flash \nflooding would be the secondary threat. Even if the MCS passes \nto the southwest, a boundary cold fire more convection late \nTuesday into Wednesday which could lead to additional flooding \nconcerns. \n\nAll this to say, this entire forecast period will remain low\nconfidence with each subsequent storm/threat chance tied to the\nprior, so monitoring the forecast frequently may be imperative.\nStorms are possible at any time which may be severe, while \nflooding seems possible (most likely for east OH, southwest PA, \nnorth WV) with isolated pockets of 2\" to 5\" possible in the next\n48 hours. Wednesday and Thursday will bring additional chances \nof severe and flash flooding, though chances of systems slowly \ndecrease through time as the development region is expected to \ndrift south into the central Appalachians.",
